.SS Why this tool is obsolete
.PP
\fBskill\fR was deprecated in upstream procps-ng in favour of
\fBpkill\fR(1), which offers regex matching against \fIcomm\fR or
the full command line, richer filters (parent PID, process group,
real vs. effective UID, run state, age), inverse matching
(\fB\-v\fR), and exact matching (\fB\-x\fR). The same use cases
expressed in \fBskill\fR's filter-only syntax (\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-u\fR,
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-p\fR) are all expressible in \fBpkill\fR with
clearer semantics.
.PP
The tool is shipped here for shell-script compatibility; new
scripts should prefer \fBpkill\fR(1) or \fBkill\fR(1) directly.
.SS Signal flag placement
.PP
The short signal form (\fB\-\fR\fISIG\fR) and the long form
(\fB\-\-signal\fR \fISIG\fR) are interchangeable. The short form may
appear at any position; \fBskill \-v \-KILL bash\fR is valid.
At most one signal flag is honoured per invocation: if multiple
appear, only the first is used and the others are treated as
unknown options.
.PP
Bare PIDs that begin with a hyphen — for example \fB\-1234\fR to
mean "process group 1234" in \fBkill\fR(1)'s convention — are not
accepted here; use \fB\-p\fR \fI1234\fR or invoke \fBkill\fR(1)
directly.
.PP
\fB\-0\fR and \fB\-\-signal 0\fR probe whether each selected process
exists and may be signalled, without delivering a signal.
.SS Permissions
.PP
\fBskill\fR uses the \fBkill\fR(2) system call. The same rules apply
as for \fBkill\fR(1) and \fBpkill\fR(1): an unprivileged caller may
only signal processes whose real or saved set-user-ID matches its
own, except for \fBSIGCONT\fR (same-session is sufficient).
Privileged callers have \fBCAP_KILL\fR in the target's user
namespace.
.SS Real-time signals
.PP
\fB\-l\fR and \fB\-L\fR currently enumerate only the standard
1..31 signals; the real-time range (\fBSIGRTMIN\fR..\fBSIGRTMAX\fR
described in \fBsignal\fR(7)) is not listed. They can still be sent
by number via \fB\-\-signal 34\fR or as a leading \fB\-34\fR.
